; Surface features: a tile stamped IN PLACE OF a cell's FLOOR or CEILING block —
; the wall-niche idea laid flat, pointing down or up. `surface` picks which.
; The tile rides that surface's variant bucket, so it wears whatever texture the
; cell wears. That is the whole point: a hole in the floor that is actually part
; of the floor, rather than a prop parked on top of one.
;
; Floor and ceiling are ONE catalog because they differ in exactly one bit. The
; RECORD keyword still names the surface (`floorfeature` / `ceilingfeature`),
; which is what lets the map parser stay catalog-blind.
;
; A feature mesh must span the full cell (x,z in [-0.5, 0.5]) with its surface at
; y = 0 and use its block's UVs (u = x + 0.5, v = z + 0.5), or the cell stops
; tiling with its neighbours. tools/BuildFloorRecess.py is the reference and
; asserts both. A CEILING tile is authored facing DOWN and rises in +y, matching
; the ceiling block (ModelBaker's BuildWornCeilingBlock).
;
; `texture` is deliberately absent — the cell supplies it.

[recess]
display = Floor Recess
model = floor_recess

; A dished drain dropping into a shaft. A POLAR LOFT (tools/BuildFloorDrain.py):
; it began as a heightfield, which suited a shallow basin and could not survive
; the throat going deep — a heightfield has one z per (x,y) so it can never be
; vertical.
[drain]
display = Floor Drain
model = floor_drain

; Broken paving: slabs settled onto a bed, two of them gone. Placed faces
; (tools/BuildFloorCracked.py) — a crack wants a crisp edge, and a grid fine
; enough for a 5 cm gap would cost thousands of verts to say "nine flat slabs".
[cracked]
display = Cracked Paving
model = floor_cracked

; --- ceiling ----------------------------------------------------------------

; A CLOISTER vault: four webs springing from the four edges of the bay to a
; crown, groins up the diagonals. A heightfield (tools/BuildCeilingVault.py) —
; it is a surface, not a shape with walls.
;
; Not a GROIN vault, which is the more obvious choice over a square bay: a groin
; vault is two crossing barrels and a barrel is OPEN AT ITS ENDS, so its boundary
; sits at crown height along two edges — exactly what a per-cell tile may not do.
; A cloister vault springs from all four edges and meets its neighbours flat.
[vault]
display = Cloister Vault
tags = temple stone
surface = ceiling
model = ceiling_vault
